          questions: [
            { q: "What problem does Data Mesh solve?", opts: ["Slow queries in databases", "The centralized data team bottleneck — domain teams own their data, eliminating the single team that all data flows through", "Cloud storage costs", "Data security gaps"], a: 1, exp: "Central data teams can't scale to serve all domains. Data Mesh distributes ownership: sales team owns sales data, logistics owns logistics data." },
            { q: "What are the 4 Data Mesh principles?", opts: ["Collect, Store, Process, Analyze", "Domain Ownership, Data as Product, Self-Serve Platform, Federated Governance", "Centralize, Standardize, Optimize, Govern", "Ingest, Transform, Store, Serve"], a: 1, exp: "Zhamak Dehghani's Data Mesh: domain teams own data as products, a platform enables self-service, and governance is federated (not centralized)." },
            { q: "What is a Data Product in Data Mesh?", opts: ["A SaaS data tool", "A high-quality, discoverable, addressable, self-describing data asset that a domain team maintains as a first-class product", "A dashboard", "A raw data export"], a: 1, exp: "Data products have SLAs, documentation, owners, and versioning. They're designed to be consumed by others — not just internal datasets." },
            { q: "When is Data Mesh appropriate?", opts: ["Always — it's the best architecture", "Large organizations with multiple domains generating data where a central team is a bottleneck", "Small startups", "Single-domain applications"], a: 1, exp: "Data Mesh adds organizational and technical complexity. It's appropriate for large, multi-domain orgs where centralized data teams can't keep pace with demand." },
            { q: "What is Federated Computational Governance in Data Mesh?", opts: ["Central team controlling all data", "Global standards (schema, security, quality) enforced computationally, with local teams making domain decisions within those standards", "No governance at all", "Governance by external auditors"], a: 1, exp: "Federated governance balances autonomy and standards. Teams choose their technology but must comply with global standards for interoperability and compliance." }
          ]
